1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:06,412
The CTO is looking at a branch.

2
00:00:06,752 --> 00:00:10,583
"Add document loader" appears in
it twice, with two different IDs.

3
00:00:10,623 --> 00:00:15,041
So does "Add text cleaner". There's a
merge commit on top, a commit with two

4
00:00:15,081 --> 00:00:17,328
parents, that nobody remembers wanting.

5
00:00:17,668 --> 00:00:22,267
Two engineers were involved. One rebased
the branch and force-pushed it, with a

6
00:00:22,307 --> 00:00:26,964
lease, exactly as the documentation
recommends. The other fetched, read what git

7
00:00:27,004 --> 00:00:31,486
status suggested, and followed the
suggestion. Each did something reasonable.

8
00:00:31,526 --> 00:00:35,772
Together they produced a history in
which every change is recorded twice.

9
00:00:36,112 --> 00:00:40,721
You're going to play both roles, watch the
duplication happen, and then repair it.

10
00:00:40,761 --> 00:00:45,484
After that you break a branch on your own,
lose the obvious way back, and recover it

11
00:00:45,524 --> 00:00:48,999
anyway. Which reasonable step
did the damage? Hold your guess.

12
00:00:50,839 --> 00:00:53,952
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:53,992 --> 00:00:57,209
Every tutorial has the sentence
"never rebase a public branch".

14
00:00:57,249 --> 00:01:01,344
You've probably repeated it. Today you
replace the slogan with the mechanism, so

15
00:01:01,384 --> 00:01:05,634
that you can also say when the rule doesn't
apply, and what to do after someone has

16
00:01:05,674 --> 00:01:06,151
broken it.

17
00:01:06,491 --> 00:01:12,841
The mechanism is the one from the first
video of this module: a rebase copies.

18
00:01:12,881 --> 00:01:19,722
It makes a new copy of each of your commits,
your saved snapshots, on a new starting

19
00:01:19,762 --> 00:01:26,521
point, and each copy has a new ID. After
you rebase and publish, your clone and the

20
00:01:26,561 --> 00:01:28,405
server hold the copies.

21
00:01:28,745 --> 00:01:33,717
Your colleague's clone, her own copy of
the repository, still holds the originals,

22
00:01:33,757 --> 00:01:37,079
with her own work on top. Git
identifies commits by ID.

23
00:01:37,119 --> 00:01:42,091
To Git, the copies and the originals are
unrelated commits that happen to make the

24
00:01:42,131 --> 00:01:42,885
same changes.

25
00:01:43,225 --> 00:01:45,861
The second half of the video
is about your own mistakes.

26
00:01:45,901 --> 00:01:49,876
A rebase destroys nothing. There are three
handles on the old state, and they differ

27
00:01:49,916 --> 00:01:53,794
in how long they last. You'll see the
shortest-lived one fail and the most durable

28
00:01:53,834 --> 00:01:54,750
one save the branch.

29
00:01:56,590 --> 00:01:58,169
Here's what you'll walk away with.

30
00:01:58,509 --> 00:01:59,921
After this video you can:

31
00:02:00,261 --> 00:02:04,943
Explain how a teammate ends up with every
commit twice after a shared branch was

32
00:02:04,983 --> 00:02:05,415
rebased.

33
00:02:05,755 --> 00:02:08,848
Repair the teammate's branch
without losing their own commit.

34
00:02:09,188 --> 00:02:13,567
Recover the pre-rebase state through ORIG_HEAD
and through the reflog, and say when

35
00:02:13,607 --> 00:02:14,419
the first fails.

36
00:02:14,759 --> 00:02:17,031
State the rule about published history
as a consequence, not as a slogan.

37
00:02:18,871 --> 00:02:24,320
Section 9.15, in one sentence. If you rebase
a branch that someone else has built on

38
00:02:24,360 --> 00:02:29,744
and publish the result, their clone holds
the old commits, the server holds the new

39
00:02:29,784 --> 00:02:34,776
ones, and Git sees two separate lines
of history that happen to make the same

40
00:02:34,816 --> 00:02:35,299
changes.

41
00:02:35,639 --> 00:02:40,439
Follow it step by step, in your teammate's
clone. Before: you pushed commits L and

42
00:02:40,479 --> 00:02:45,102
C. She fetched them and committed K on
top. origin/feat/ingest is her record of

43
00:02:45,142 --> 00:02:47,109
where the branch is on the server.

44
00:02:47,449 --> 00:02:51,423
You rebase: L and C become L-prime
and C-prime, on a new base.

45
00:02:51,463 --> 00:02:56,602
You force-push, replacing the commits on
the server's branch: the server now has

46
00:02:56,642 --> 00:03:01,782
L-prime and C-prime. Her clone has L, C,
K. When she fetches, her branch and its

47
00:03:01,822 --> 00:03:07,220
upstream have diverged. If she merges, the
merge keeps the commits of both sides: L,

48
00:03:07,260 --> 00:03:10,263
C, K, and L-prime, C-prime.
Every change twice.

49
00:03:10,603 --> 00:03:14,955
The root cause, as the textbook states
it: Git identifies a commit by its ID.

50
00:03:14,995 --> 00:03:19,633
Your copies have new IDs, so for Git they're
different commits from the originals,

51
00:03:19,673 --> 00:03:22,143
and a merge keeps the
commits of both sides.

52
00:03:22,483 --> 00:03:27,995
Is the content duplicated? No. The merge's
diff is small, because both lines make

53
00:03:28,035 --> 00:03:31,011
the same changes. The
history is duplicated.

54
00:03:31,351 --> 00:03:36,554
Why is that more than noise? Every later
reader of the history meets each change

55
00:03:36,594 --> 00:03:41,665
twice. Worse: if she pushes her merge,
the commits you removed are back on the

56
00:03:41,705 --> 00:03:47,038
server. When the purpose of the rewrite
was to remove something, a credential or a

57
00:03:47,078 --> 00:03:51,625
large file, one stale clone that
merges and pushes undoes the cleanup.

58
00:03:51,965 --> 00:03:56,596
The repair is on her side: go back to
before the merge, then replay only her own

59
00:03:56,636 --> 00:03:58,290
commit onto the new upstream.

60
00:03:58,630 --> 00:04:03,601
Now the rule. Pro Git states it as: "Do
not rebase commits that exist outside your

61
00:04:03,641 --> 00:04:06,718
repository and that people
may have based work on."

62
00:04:06,758 --> 00:04:11,729
The textbook gives it a working form, a
question to ask before you rebase anything

63
00:04:11,769 --> 00:04:14,968
you have pushed: who else
has commits on top of this?

64
00:04:15,008 --> 00:04:19,429
If nobody, as on the branch of your
own pull request: rebase and publish.

65
00:04:19,469 --> 00:04:24,379
If somebody: agree on it first and send
them the repair command, or don't rebase,

66
00:04:24,419 --> 00:04:25,479
and merge instead.

67
00:04:25,819 --> 00:04:31,194
Section 9.16, in one sentence. A rebase
destroys nothing: the old tip is named by

68
00:04:31,234 --> 00:04:36,810
ORIG_HEAD until another command overwrites
that ref, and by the reflog of the branch

69
00:04:36,850 --> 00:04:41,958
for weeks. The reflog is Git's local
list of every position a branch has had.

70
00:04:42,298 --> 00:04:46,816
How long is "until the entry expires"?
The textbook gives the defaults.

71
00:04:46,856 --> 00:04:51,952
A reflog entry whose commit is no longer
reachable from the branch tip, which is

72
00:04:51,992 --> 00:04:55,226
what a rebase leaves behind,
expires after 30 days.

73
00:04:55,266 --> 00:04:59,142
The unreachable objects are
pruned after a further two weeks.

74
00:04:59,182 --> 00:05:04,214
The lab configuration sets the reflog
never to expire, so the transcripts don't

75
00:05:04,254 --> 00:05:05,433
depend on the date.

76
00:05:07,273 --> 00:05:11,921
The textbook's analogy: you reprint chapters
1 and 2 of a shared manuscript with

77
00:05:11,961 --> 00:05:16,608
corrections and replace the copies in the
office. Your co-author is at home with

78
00:05:16,648 --> 00:05:21,354
chapter 3 stapled to the old chapters 1
and 2. If she staples the new print on as

79
00:05:21,394 --> 00:05:24,459
well, the manuscript contains
chapters 1 and 2 twice.

80
00:05:24,799 --> 00:05:29,280
It breaks at one point: a person
would recognise the duplicate text.

81
00:05:29,320 --> 00:05:34,865
Git, which identifies commits by ID, does
not, unless you ask it to compare patches.

82
00:05:34,905 --> 00:05:40,450
That "unless" is the tool for diagnosis:
--cherry-mark compares patch IDs, hashes of

83
00:05:40,490 --> 00:05:43,375
the changes themselves,
and marks the pairs.

84
00:05:43,715 --> 00:05:48,817
For recovery, the model is a ladder with
three rungs, and each rung lasts longer

85
00:05:48,857 --> 00:05:52,738
than the one above it. --abort
exists only during the rebase.

86
00:05:52,778 --> 00:05:56,273
ORIG_HEAD exists until the
next command that writes it.

87
00:05:56,313 --> 00:06:01,287
The reflog entry exists for weeks. When
a rung is gone, step down to the next.

88
00:06:03,127 --> 00:06:07,563
Three repositories, and after your push
the originals L and C exist as branch

89
00:06:07,603 --> 00:06:09,714
history in exactly one of them: hers.

90
00:06:11,554 --> 00:06:13,730
Part 1: you rebase and publish.

91
00:06:14,070 --> 00:06:18,387
git rebase main 🟡 CAUTION. Two
commits replayed. Then a plain push.

92
00:06:18,727 --> 00:06:23,234
Rejected, non-fast-forward: the server's
tip is not an ancestor of yours.

93
00:06:23,274 --> 00:06:28,218
Read the hint: it recommends git pull.
After a rebase of your own that advice is

94
00:06:28,258 --> 00:06:33,388
wrong. A pull would bring the old commits
back, merged next to your copies or, with

95
00:06:33,428 --> 00:06:35,070
--rebase, in place of them.

96
00:06:35,410 --> 00:06:39,172
The second command is a forced push
🔴 DANGEROUS, with both guards.

97
00:06:39,212 --> 00:06:43,549
The lease means: only if the server's
branch is still where you last saw it.

98
00:06:43,589 --> 00:06:46,544
The five answers are in the
video on forcing a push.

99
00:06:46,584 --> 00:06:50,979
The one that matters most today is "what
can it destroy": every commit on the

100
00:06:51,019 --> 00:06:55,817
server's branch that is not in your history.
Here that's your own two originals, and

101
00:06:55,857 --> 00:07:00,368
you meant to replace them. What you didn't
check is who else had built on them.

102
00:07:00,708 --> 00:07:02,353
Part 2: in Asha's clone.

103
00:07:02,693 --> 00:07:10,084
Her branch: 1279adf, dc5df93,
and her own c60bc13 on top.

104
00:07:10,424 --> 00:07:15,447
"(forced update)" on the fetch line. And
status: diverged, "3 and 3 different

105
00:07:15,487 --> 00:07:19,393
commits each". Her side has the
two old commits and her own.

106
00:07:19,433 --> 00:07:23,075
The other side has the README
commit and the two copies.

107
00:07:23,115 --> 00:07:26,495
Status suggests git pull.
She does it, with a merge.

108
00:07:26,535 --> 00:07:29,717
Predict the graph. Draw
it if you can. I'll wait.

109
00:07:34,557 --> 00:07:41,784
Two parallel lines joined by a merge. "Add
document loader" on the left as 1279adf

110
00:07:41,824 --> 00:07:48,342
and on the right as 0fbfd81. "Add text
cleaner" as dc5df93 and as 8aca274.

111
00:07:48,382 --> 00:07:55,520
That's the CTO's branch from the opening,
and the step that built it was the pull

112
00:07:55,560 --> 00:07:57,469
that status suggested.

113
00:07:57,809 --> 00:08:03,159
--cherry-mark puts an equals sign on four
commits: two pairs with the same patch.

114
00:08:03,499 --> 00:08:08,256
Her own commit is marked with a less-than
sign and the README with a greater-than

115
00:08:08,296 --> 00:08:12,993
sign. And the diff from before the merge
to after it shows one file, the README.

116
00:08:13,033 --> 00:08:15,717
The content is not
duplicated, the history is.

117
00:08:16,057 --> 00:08:21,331
Part 3: the repair, on her side. Go back
to before the merge with git reset --hard

118
00:08:21,371 --> 00:08:25,284
ORIG_HEAD 🔴. ORIG_HEAD names
where HEAD was before the merge.

119
00:08:25,324 --> 00:08:30,533
It's the very next state-changing command
after the merge, her tree is clean, and

120
00:08:30,573 --> 00:08:34,550
the merge hasn't been pushed.
Then replay only her own commit.

121
00:08:34,890 --> 00:08:40,301
Look at the middle command. git merge-base
--fork-point looks into the reflog of

122
00:08:40,341 --> 00:08:45,956
origin/feat/ingest, finds that her branch
left that remote-tracking branch when its

123
00:08:45,996 --> 00:08:51,475
tip was dc5df93, and so identifies "Add
chunker" as the only commit that is hers.

124
00:08:51,515 --> 00:08:55,494
git pull --rebase uses the same
logic: one commit replayed.

125
00:08:55,834 --> 00:09:00,153
Her chunker, now fbce73d, sits
on top of your two copies.

126
00:09:00,193 --> 00:09:01,989
One line, nothing twice.

127
00:09:02,329 --> 00:09:07,445
You saw this fork-point logic do harm
in the video on forced pushes, where it

128
00:09:07,485 --> 00:09:13,002
dropped a commit the teammate had pushed.
Here it does good, because her commit was

129
00:09:13,042 --> 00:09:18,359
never pushed. Same rule, different history.
The explicit form, git rebase --onto

130
00:09:18,399 --> 00:09:21,172
origin/feat/ingest dc5df93, is in Lab 9.6.

131
00:09:21,512 --> 00:09:25,291
Part 4: your own bad rebase.
labs/run ch09/recover.

132
00:09:25,331 --> 00:09:31,432
The plan was to drop two experiment commits.
The wrong lines were deleted from the

133
00:09:31,472 --> 00:09:32,181
todo list.

134
00:09:35,521 --> 00:09:39,406
The saved list has three lines: the
skeleton and the two experiments.

135
00:09:39,446 --> 00:09:43,161
The two commits you wanted to keep
are gone, and Git said nothing.

136
00:09:43,501 --> 00:09:47,216
They're gone from the branch,
not from the repository.

137
00:09:47,256 --> 00:09:52,502
ORIG_HEAD still names the old tip,
6ab8f55. Noticed at once, the undo is one

138
00:09:52,542 --> 00:09:53,058
command.

139
00:09:53,398 --> 00:09:58,615
git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD 🔴. From the
textbook: it moves the current branch to that

140
00:09:58,655 --> 00:10:02,432
commit and overwrites the index
and the working tree with it.

141
00:10:02,472 --> 00:10:07,439
It destroys uncommitted changes to tracked
files. Preview with git status, which

142
00:10:07,479 --> 00:10:12,383
should be clean, as it is after a finished
rebase, and git diff ORIG_HEAD HEAD.

143
00:10:12,423 --> 00:10:17,327
What it moves away from stays recoverable:
the rebased tip is now <branch>@{1}.

144
00:10:17,667 --> 00:10:23,339
Five commits again. Now the same mistake,
not noticed, followed by a reset for

145
00:10:23,379 --> 00:10:29,270
another reason. Quick quiz, two options.
Afterwards, ORIG_HEAD names: option one,

146
00:10:29,310 --> 00:10:33,664
the good tip, 6ab8f55. Option
two, the damaged tip, e30ba74.

147
00:10:33,704 --> 00:10:34,835
Say it out loud.

148
00:10:44,875 --> 00:10:49,992
Option two. e30ba74: the tip before the
reset, which is the damaged branch.

149
00:10:50,032 --> 00:10:55,768
No name on screen points at the two good
commits. The first rung is gone, the second

150
00:10:55,808 --> 00:10:58,862
has been overwritten.
Step down to the third.

151
00:10:59,202 --> 00:11:04,492
Read it from the top: a reset, the second
bad rebase, the reset to ORIG_HEAD, the

152
00:11:04,532 --> 00:11:08,374
first bad rebase, and below
that the five original commits.

153
00:11:08,414 --> 00:11:13,835
Try it now. Thirty seconds: find the entry
that is the last good state, and say its

154
00:11:13,875 --> 00:11:14,888
number out loud.

155
00:11:19,728 --> 00:11:23,655
feat/rerank@{2}: 6ab8f55,
"reset: moving to ORIG_HEAD".

156
00:11:23,695 --> 00:11:27,911
There is one entry per rebase,
so you count in the listing.

157
00:11:27,951 --> 00:11:32,888
If you picked entry 4, look at its
ID: the same commit, further down.

158
00:11:40,428 --> 00:11:44,608
The rescue branch comes first for a reason.
It's a ref, a name that points at a

159
00:11:44,648 --> 00:11:48,935
commit, so the commits stay reachable
whatever you type next, and you can inspect

160
00:11:48,975 --> 00:11:51,927
them before moving the real
branch. Only then the reset.

161
00:11:52,267 --> 00:11:56,585
The reflog of HEAD holds the same story
in more detail, one line per step.

162
00:11:56,625 --> 00:12:01,532
For "the branch before its last rebase",
the branch's own reflog is the shorter list

163
00:12:01,572 --> 00:12:02,003
to read.

164
00:12:03,843 --> 00:12:05,491
Five mistakes to watch for.

165
00:12:05,831 --> 00:12:09,648
Following the git pull hint after
your own rebase was rejected on push.

166
00:12:09,688 --> 00:12:13,940
Root cause: the server still has the
originals; a pull merges them next to your

167
00:12:13,980 --> 00:12:16,167
copies or replaces your copies with them.

168
00:12:16,507 --> 00:12:19,718
Merging after a "(forced update)"
on a branch you have built on.

169
00:12:19,758 --> 00:12:23,629
Root cause: commits are identified by
ID, so the copies and the originals are

170
00:12:23,669 --> 00:12:26,067
different commits, and a
merge keeps both lines.

171
00:12:26,407 --> 00:12:31,005
Pushing that merge. Root cause: it makes
the removed commits reachable on the server

172
00:12:31,045 --> 00:12:34,759
again, which undoes a rewrite whose
purpose was to remove something.

173
00:12:35,099 --> 00:12:40,889
Relying on ORIG_HEAD hours after a rebase.
Root cause: it is one slot, rewritten by

174
00:12:40,929 --> 00:12:43,699
the next reset, merge, rebase or git am.

175
00:12:44,039 --> 00:12:47,156
Moving the real branch before
creating a rescue ref.

176
00:12:47,196 --> 00:12:52,014
Root cause: until a ref points at the
recovered commits, they are reachable only

177
00:12:52,054 --> 00:12:55,840
through reflog entries that further
commands keep pushing down.

178
00:12:57,680 --> 00:13:02,286
Now, out of the lab. Picture a team that
shares a long-running branch for an

179
00:13:02,326 --> 00:13:07,421
ingestion pipeline. On a Friday one engineer
rebases it onto main "to keep it fresh"

180
00:13:07,461 --> 00:13:11,822
and force-pushes with a lease. The
lease holds: nobody had pushed since.

181
00:13:11,862 --> 00:13:15,613
Two colleagues had unpushed
commits on top of the old history.

182
00:13:15,653 --> 00:13:20,259
On Monday both pull. One gets a merge
with every commit twice and pushes it.

183
00:13:20,299 --> 00:13:25,088
By Tuesday the branch on the server
contains the old line, the new line and two

184
00:13:25,128 --> 00:13:25,516
merges.

185
00:13:25,856 --> 00:13:30,470
Nothing in that story required a mistake
by Git or a missing guard on the push.

186
00:13:30,510 --> 00:13:34,358
The lease protects the server's
ref. It says nothing about clones.

187
00:13:34,698 --> 00:13:39,623
So the control is the textbook's working
question, asked before the rebase: who else

188
00:13:39,663 --> 00:13:44,294
has commits on top of this? If the answer
is "somebody", either the team agrees

189
00:13:44,334 --> 00:13:49,260
first and everybody receives the repair
command, or the branch is brought up to date

190
00:13:49,300 --> 00:13:50,028
with a merge.

191
00:13:50,368 --> 00:13:55,568
On the platform side, according to the
documentation, a new ruleset, GitHub's named

192
00:13:55,608 --> 00:14:00,871
list of rules for branches, has "Block force
pushes" enabled by default, and classic

193
00:14:00,911 --> 00:14:04,406
branch protection rules disable
force pushes by default.

194
00:14:04,446 --> 00:14:09,583
The textbook's advice: use that for main
and for release branches, and leave topic

195
00:14:09,623 --> 00:14:11,477
branches open to their owners.

196
00:14:11,817 --> 00:14:16,438
And for recovery: it's local. If you had
already force-pushed a damaged branch,

197
00:14:16,478 --> 00:14:19,860
restore it in your clone first,
and then publish it again.

198
00:14:21,700 --> 00:14:28,098
Your turn. Do Lab 9.5, "Break a
branch on purpose and recover it", in

199
00:14:28,138 --> 00:14:30,430
lab-manual/m09-rebase.md.

200
00:14:30,770 --> 00:14:32,122
Predict before each step:

201
00:14:32,462 --> 00:14:36,836
After the bad rebase: which commits are
missing, and what does ORIG_HEAD name?

202
00:14:37,176 --> 00:14:41,220
After the failure step of the lab:
what does ORIG_HEAD name now?

203
00:14:41,560 --> 00:14:45,602
In the branch's reflog: which entry
number is the last good state?

204
00:14:45,642 --> 00:14:47,211
Count before you print it.

205
00:14:47,551 --> 00:14:50,215
Create the rescue branch
before you move anything.

206
00:14:50,555 --> 00:14:54,205
The challenge is Lab 9.6, "Rebase a shared
branch and watch a teammate's history

207
00:14:54,245 --> 00:14:57,987
duplicate", in the same file. Play both
roles, and write down what each person saw

208
00:14:58,027 --> 00:14:58,587
at each step.

209
00:15:00,427 --> 00:15:05,470
Q155: "You force-pushed a rebased branch.
A teammate now has every commit twice.

210
00:15:05,510 --> 00:15:10,235
Explain how that happened, how they
repair it, and what you would have done

211
00:15:10,275 --> 00:15:11,062
differently."

212
00:15:13,402 --> 00:15:17,579
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
has three parts in the order asked.

213
00:15:17,619 --> 00:15:22,367
The explanation follows the commits through
three repositories and rests on one fact

214
00:15:22,407 --> 00:15:26,927
about how Git identifies a commit. The
repair is given from the teammate's seat,

215
00:15:26,967 --> 00:15:31,658
with the state she must return to first and
how her own commits are told apart from

216
00:15:31,698 --> 00:15:36,103
the old copies. The third part is not "I
would not have rebased": it names the

217
00:15:36,143 --> 00:15:40,549
question you would have asked, and the
two acceptable courses depending on its

218
00:15:40,589 --> 00:15:40,948
answer.

219
00:15:42,788 --> 00:15:46,915
Let's land this. You should now be
able to say, in your own words:

220
00:15:47,255 --> 00:15:52,254
A rebase copies. So after I publish a
rebased branch, a teammate who built on the

221
00:15:52,294 --> 00:15:56,609
old commits holds originals that Git
treats as unrelated to my copies.

222
00:15:56,649 --> 00:16:01,586
A merge then keeps both lines, and pushing
it brings the old commits back to the

223
00:16:01,626 --> 00:16:06,500
server. The repair is on their side:
return to before the merge and replay only

224
00:16:06,540 --> 00:16:11,663
their own commits onto the new tip. Before
rebasing anything pushed, I ask who else

225
00:16:11,703 --> 00:16:13,219
has commits on top of it.

226
00:16:13,559 --> 00:16:19,161
My own bad rebase destroys nothing: --abort
works while it runs, ORIG_HEAD until the

227
00:16:19,201 --> 00:16:23,392
next command that writes it, and
the branch's reflog for weeks.

228
00:16:23,432 --> 00:16:26,885
I create a rescue branch
before I move the real one.

229
00:16:28,725 --> 00:16:33,150
Read sections 9.15 and 9.16 of Chapter 9.

230
00:16:33,490 --> 00:16:38,842
Today you played both sides of a duplicated
history, and you recovered a branch from

231
00:16:38,882 --> 00:16:43,078
its reflog. Break one on purpose
in Lab 9.5 before the next video.

232
00:16:43,118 --> 00:16:47,635
Next time: publishing a rebased branch,
rebase or merge, and the rebase

233
00:16:47,675 --> 00:16:51,936
configuration. Until then, look at
the state first and type second.

234
00:16:51,976 --> 00:16:53,476
See you in the next one.
